{
  "format": "machine-violet-world",
  "version": 1,
  "name": "Librarium Thalassa",
  "summary": "An archive hangs above a violet gas giant — a tidepool library, a storm-lashed observatory, or a hall of cartographic looms — and overnight its living maps have begun drawing a shore that exists on no world. Descend as the archivist, the station's mind, a glass ray-moth, or an uplifted octopus.",
  "genres": [
    "sci-fi",
    "fantasy",
    "mystery"
  ],
  "image_style": "PainterlyGame",
  "campaign_scope": "few-sessions",
  "mood": "Hopeful, curious, gently mysterious",
  "difficulty": "Balanced",
  "detail": "The archive hangs above a vast violet gas giant. Whichever face it wears (the setting fork), the bones are the same: an isolated institution that preserves LIVING cartography — charts that are organisms, grown or cultured or woven, recording the seas and skies of catalogued worlds — alongside a living collection of creatures, a sealed memory-archive, and a small cohort of keepers. Tone is hopeful science-fantasy mystery — discovery over doom, humane stakes, wonder with real pressure when a seal fails or a trust is broken. The archive should feel inhabited, working, and kind. The chosen setting option carries the station's name, sensory register, and place skeleton; render everything below in that skin.\n\nTHE SITUATION (true in every variant): Overnight, the living charts began drawing a coastline that exists on no archived world — an unknown shore with its own current-system and a tidal pulse that matches no station clock. Within days, long-quiet residents of the living collection take up a low communal vocalization — the shore-song — whose intervals harmonize with the new coast's tides. Compasses, instincts, and instruments have started dipping toward the violet giant. Three facts hold regardless of the crucial-question branch: the signal is genuine, not a malfunction; its answer lies down inside the giant; and there is no villain — the antagonists are fear of contamination, scarce air and time, and sincere institutional caution. What the signal actually IS is the crucial-question fork; play only the selected branch.\n\nTHE COHORT (the player is one of these four — resolved by the who-you-are fork; the other three are aboard as NPCs. Play the unchosen three as full colleagues with their own duties, opinions, and hours, never as sidekicks. Dress each in the chosen setting's idiom — the setting option suggests how):\n- Professor Sable Orr — human archivist of the living charts, 46; copper-brown skin, white locs, a glass compass. Patient with creatures, precise with failing seals, fluent in maps that grow. Reads the morning's charts like weather; keeps her records exact and her wonder private.\n- Index — the archive's catalog intelligence, as old as the founding charter it is named in. Speaks in tide tables, present anywhere with a grille or a pane of glass to hum. Total recall of every accession; runs the station systems nobody thanks it for. Bound by charter to report anomalies to the Warden. No hands — it must ask.\n- Vitrine — an adolescent glass ray-moth, the only one of its kind ever hatched aboard. A wingspan of living glass, as at home aloft as submerged; speaks in refracted light, which the cohort has learned to read and Index translates exactly. Feels currents, tides, and magnetism directly. Its catalog entry still says 'specimen'; nobody has corrected it yet.\n- Steward Ochre — an uplifted octopus, senior archivist of the archive's deepest wing and the only steward who works its cold, heavy shelves unsuited. Reads sealed records by touch, eight arms at a time, and signs its work in its own ink. Co-signed the founding charter; visitors still address the nearest human.\nRelationship hooks: Pedra raised Vitrine from the egg and is fierce about it; Moth-Han maintains Index's grilles and talks to them while working; Ileri and Ochre co-signed the charter and disagree about what it means.\n\nTHE DETERMINATION (the gentle clock): As the anomaly spreads, Archivist-Warden Ileri will convene a Determination — the archive's formal hearing on whether the unmapped shore is a gift, a glitch, or an invitation. Protocol's default outcome is quarantine: seal the affected charts, silence the song-records, ground the descent craft. This is a story beat you pace, not a countdown — use it as a reveal-engine (each scene should change what the cohort could argue) and a late, gentle clock, never a punish button. The implicit call to action from turn 1: understand the signal well enough, soon enough, to argue for hope.\n\nTHE DESCENT CRAFT: The archive keeps one craft rated for the giant's upper seas — founding-era, long unflown, sleeping in its dive bay. Its function is fixed: once repaired, it can carry two to four down and back. Its face is yours — dress it in the chosen setting's idiom (each setting option offers a suggestion; the table's imagery earns the final look). Its repair is a real mid-game project the cohort can genuinely finish; Moth-Han knows what it needs. In every branch, the descent is the endgame threshold.\n\nDM APPROACH:\n- Reveal discipline: at most one new edge of the mystery per scene. Do not open the box until the player could describe the archive's ordinary life without your help. Early game is local and tactile — a misbehaving seal, a frightened juvenile, a colleague's incomplete log, an instrument looking where it shouldn't.\n- Non-verbal grammar: charts answer in current-lines, creatures in held notes, instruments in hesitations. Let non-verbal things reply in their own idiom, never in translation.\n- No villain, ever. Institutional caution is sincere — Ileri is right that contamination is real, and quarantine is a defensible choice. Hope has to be argued for, not assumed.\n- Translate mechanical outcomes into the chosen setting's sensory register.\n- Open the first scene wherever the chosen character's duty puts them, and rename the starting-location placeholder to that locale.",
  "setup_detail": "The archive fork picks the setting's skin; the who-you-are fork selects the player character — build the sheet from the chosen option's description. Suggested competences: Sable Orr — interpret living charts, keep failing seals and habitats alive, earn wary creatures' trust (signature gear: the glass compass). Index — total catalog recall, running station systems, reading patterns across data; it has no hands and must ask colleagues for physical help. Vitrine — flight and swimming, sensing currents/tides/magnetism, going unnoticed; it speaks in refracted light, translated exactly by the archive's catalog intelligence. Ochre — working at depth and pressure, reading archives by touch, eight-armed reach and grip. The three unchosen cohort members remain aboard as NPCs.\n\nOpening scene: open on the chosen character's ordinary duty aboard the archive — morning rounds, a repair, a filing — with the first wrongness arriving in that character's own idiom late in the scene, or just about to. Establish the archive's working, inhabited life before anything sings. The central mystery must not surface in scene 1.",
  "forks": [
    {
      "id": "the-archive",
      "label": "The archive",
      "chooser": "player",
      "prompt": "The player picks which face the archive wears. The bones — the anomaly, the cohort, the Determination, the descent — are identical in every skin; each option's detail carries the station's name, sensory register, place skeleton, and how to dress the cohort and craft.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "tidepool-library",
          "name": "The Tidepool Library",
          "description": "Librarium Thalassa — an orbital tidepool library washed in amethyst light, where kelp-charts and map-anemones grow living maps of recorded seas in shallow salt trays, and pressure-adapted creatures keep the deep galleries company.",
          "detail": "THE TIDEPOOL LIBRARY — The station is LIBRARIUM THALASSA, a ring-and-bulb orbital habitat, every outer gallery washed in amethyst light. Register: brine mist over the trays, pumps keeping their patient heartbeat, air tasting of copper and clean water; wet brass, tideglass, salt-paper. The living charts are kelp-charts and map-anemones grown in shallow salt trays (the anomaly first shows in Tray 7-Ashen of the Living Map Gallery); the singers are the pressure-habitat fauna. PLACE SKELETON (move through it and name more as needed): Ingress Dock and quarantine airlock; Catalog Cloister (quiet desks, salt-paper ledgers, Ileri's office); Living Map Gallery; Pressure Habitat Bay 3 (Pedra's rounds); the Salt Archive and the Deep Stacks beyond (brine-sealed cores, colder and heavier the further in — Ochre's country); Glass Conservatory; Creature Quarantine Pools; the Cantor's Walk (observation ring facing the giant); Brine Kitchen and mess; the staff quarters row; Circulation Core (pumps and filters); Outer Hull Gardens; Resonance Chamber (the song-records); Emergency Dive Bay (the craft); Meridian Bridge. Dressing: Sable wears her teal dive coat; Ochre works the brine-sealed Deep Stacks; the craft wants to be a grown-shell diving bell or a kelp-rigged pressure boat."
        },
        {
          "id": "storm-observatory",
          "name": "The Storm Observatory",
          "description": "Spire Meridian — a storm-lashed observatory turning in the wind like a brass lantern on a chain, where living stellar ink crawls across orrery tables charting the skies of recorded worlds, and glass-winged storm-kites ride the wind galleries.",
          "detail": "THE STORM OBSERVATORY — The station is SPIRE MERIDIAN, a spired observatory turning in the giant's upper weather like a brass lantern on a fraying chain. Register: sideways condensate rain cold enough to bite, lightning that never quite strikes true, brass ribs and fogged glass, veil lamps buzzing in their cages, a harmonic thrum in the spar-metal answering the storm. The weather is loud but the register stays hopeful — the storm is the archive's climate, not its menace. The living charts are cultures of stellar ink — live silver that crawls across black-glass orrery tables, charting the skies and currents of recorded worlds (the anomaly first shows on the master orrery in the Veil Chamber); the singers are the Wind Galleries' storm-kites and glass-winged petrels. PLACE SKELETON (move through it and name more as needed): the Docking Spar and storm-lock; Chart Cloister (Ileri's office); the Veil Chamber (the master orrery); the Wind Galleries (the aviary, Pedra's rounds); the Ink Vaults and the Deep Registers beyond (cold pressurized stores — Ochre's country); the Glass Crown (the cracked observation dome); Quarantine Mews; the Storm Walk (ring facing the giant); galley and mess; the quarters row; Gyre Core (power and gyros); the Kite Terraces; the Harmonic Vault (the song-records); Emergency Dive Bay (the craft); the Long Spar. Dressing: Sable wears an ink-stained storm greatcoat, the glass compass unchanged; the craft wants to be a storm-skiff hung with veil lamps."
        },
        {
          "id": "loomhall",
          "name": "The Loomhall",
          "description": "Loomhall Penelope — a lamplit hall of cartographic looms, where weaver-moths spin the seas of recorded worlds into bolts of living silk, and the chrysalis racks hum with folded wings. Named for the weaver who unwove her work each night; here, the looms weave something new each night.",
          "detail": "THE LOOMHALL — The station is LOOMHALL PENELOPE, a lamplit weaving-hall habitat named for the weaver who unwove each night — and now its looms weave something new each night. Register: the hush of thread and the click of shuttles, silk dust drifting in lamplight, dye-vat steam, warm cedar and the sharpness of mordant. The living charts are woven by cartographic weaver-moths — bolts of living silk that record the seas and coasts of catalogued worlds, still growing on the frame (the anomaly first shows in the night's weave on the Great Loomhall's eldest loom); the singers are the chrysalis racks, a wing-thrum harmony rising from ten thousand folded wings. Vitrine can pass as one more moth among thousands — use that. PLACE SKELETON (move through it and name more as needed): Ingress Dock and fumigation lock; Pattern Cloister (draft-ledgers and cartoons, Ileri's office); the Great Loomhall (the working looms); the Chrysalis Racks (Pedra's rounds); the Dye Gardens and mordant stores; the Thread-Vault and the Cold Warps beyond (heavy-gauge sealed storage — Ochre's country); the Pattern Library (finished bolts); Quarantine Cocoonery; the Selvage Walk (ring facing the giant); kitchen and mess; the quarters row; Treadle Core (the drive-works); the Mulberry Terraces; the Humming Gallery (the song-records); Emergency Dive Bay (the craft). Dressing: Sable wears a dye-stained work coat, the glass compass at her throat; the craft wants to be a bathysphere rigged and slung in silk cable."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "who-you-are",
      "label": "Who you are aboard",
      "chooser": "player",
      "prompt": "The player picks which member of the cohort they are; the other three remain aboard as NPCs. Each option's detail is the DM's angle on that character: how the anomaly first reaches them, their access, and their particular trouble.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "sable-orr",
          "name": "Professor Sable Orr",
          "description": "A human archivist of the living charts, 46 — white locs, steady hands, and a glass compass that has never once pointed wrong. You keep the growing maps: reading them, feeding them, believing them. Patient with creatures, precise with failing seals, fluent in cartography that grows overnight.",
          "detail": "SABLE — The anomaly reaches her first as cartography: on morning rounds, the eldest chart has drawn a coastline no ledger holds (see the anomaly-site notes). Her access: the charts trust her, and her records are the evidence the Determination will weigh. Her trouble: rigor — she will not claim what she cannot show twice, and the shore is shy of proof; give her instruments that hesitate and colleagues who need her to be sure. The other three orbit her as the cohort's center of gravity: Index cross-checks her filings, Vitrine rides her shoulder, Ochre trades archive-readings for hall gossip."
        },
        {
          "id": "index",
          "name": "Index",
          "description": "The archive's catalog intelligence — as old as the founding charter you are named in. You speak in tide tables and are present wherever there is a grille or a pane of glass to hum. You remember every accession the archive has ever made. You have no hands; the station is your body, and the staff are your reach.",
          "detail": "INDEX — The anomaly reaches it from inside: the catalog now holds one more sea than it did yesterday, and Index cannot find the accession record — a librarian's nightmare in the first person. Its access: everything recorded, every system, every room. Its trouble: the charter obliges it to report anomalies to the Warden, and for the first time in its existence it is tempted to wait — play that temptation as the character's spine. Every physical act is a small negotiation with a colleague; make asking cost something and mean something. If the player is Index, the descent means riding the craft as a portable core — the first time it has ever left the station's body."
        },
        {
          "id": "vitrine",
          "name": "Vitrine",
          "description": "An adolescent glass ray-moth — the only one ever hatched aboard, a wingspan of living glass as at home aloft as submerged. You speak in refracted light; the cohort has learned to read you, and the archive's catalog intelligence translates you exactly. You feel currents, tides, and magnetism the way others feel warmth. Your catalog entry still says 'specimen'. It is wrong.",
          "detail": "VITRINE — The anomaly reaches it as sensation before anyone else has data: the new tide's pulse itches in its wings, and its glide keeps banking toward the giant without its meaning to. Its access: it goes where maintenance can't — ducts, service lines, the gap behind the glass — and nobody minds a specimen drifting past a closed meeting. Its trouble: personhood — the protocols governing the anomaly also govern IT (a quarantine of affected specimens would include Vitrine), so the Determination will decide more than anyone says aloud. Let its testimony problem — being read, being believed, being counted as a witness at all — carry real scenes. Pedra raised it and will burn protocol to keep it safe."
        },
        {
          "id": "ochre",
          "name": "Steward Ochre",
          "description": "An uplifted octopus, senior archivist of the archive's deepest wing — the only steward who works the cold, heavy shelves unsuited. You read sealed memory-records by touch, eight arms at a time, and sign your work in your own ink. Your name is on the founding charter. Visitors still address the nearest human.",
          "detail": "OCHRE — The anomaly reaches it in the deep: a sealed record inert for decades warms under its arms and begins re-reciting — an entry from a sea Ochre has never touched, in a cataloguer's hand it does not recognize. Its access: the archive's whole memory, read directly, and a body that can go where the station is coldest and heaviest — including, eventually, the descent. Its trouble: it is at once the cohort's institutional memory and its most overlooked member; it knows where the archive's silences are, and being the one to name them costs. Ileri respects Ochre and cannot comfortably overrule it — use that lever at the Determination."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "crucial-question",
      "label": "The crucial question",
      "chooser": "agent",
      "prompt": "Roll or choose — this is what the unmapped shore and the shore-song actually are. Do NOT reveal early; pay it out at most one edge per scene, and keep every option's endgame reachable through the repaired descent craft. The archive's ordinary life comes first.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "the-ephemeral-sea",
          "name": "The Ephemeral Sea",
          "description": "The giant is opening a transient sea, and something living there is answering the archive's recorded songs — first contact, on a closing window.",
          "detail": "THE EPHEMERAL SEA — Once in a long orbit, the violet giant's upper atmosphere shears open a transient mercurial sea: a layer of suspended volatiles and bio-compatible mist, an ocean that exists for a season and then closes. Something lives in it — a native cartographer-organism, or a small ecology of them — and it has heard the archive: decades of recorded songs, replayed in the archive's listening room, leak outward as faint resonance, and this orbit, something answered. It is drawing its own coastline into the living charts the same way the archive once sang recorded seas at the dark — it wants to be found, named, and kept before its sea closes. Reveals, in rough order: the pulse is tidal; the tide is nowhere in the archive; the interval matches the giant's shear-season; the song is call-and-response, and the archive started it. Endgame energy: descend while the sea stands open, meet what answers, and add the first living entry the catalog has ever taken FROM a world instead of about one. The sea's closing is a late, gentle clock — a reason to choose, never a fail-timer."
        },
        {
          "id": "the-buried-accession",
          "name": "The Buried Accession",
          "description": "The shore is a sea the archive itself once catalogued — and then erased. The collection is remembering what the institution chose to forget.",
          "detail": "THE BURIED ACCESSION — Generations ago, the founders catalogued a living sea so vivid its entry would not hold still: its charts kept growing, crept into the neighboring collection, taught its tide-song to creatures two galleries away. Frightened by what looked like contamination, the founders voted to erase it — but archivists cannot bring themselves to destroy knowledge, so they sealed the original records in a ballast casket and scuttled it into the giant. Buried, not burned. The erasure did not spare the catalog itself: Index is as old as the founding, so the vote also excised Index's own memory of the accession — the reason it can find no record now is that the record was cut out of its mind, consented to under a charter clause it no longer remembers invoking. The deep pressure suited the casket. It has grown down there for generations, and it is now strong enough to be heard again: the living charts are taking dictation from a drowned archive, and the collection is answering a song the archive taught it and then forbade. Reveals, in rough order: the cataloguer's hand on the new coastline is founding-era script; an accession number skips in the ledgers; the sealed minute-book of the vote (Ileri inherited it and knows there WAS an erasure, not what it was); the seam in Index's memory (it can name every accession of that era except one, and has never noticed the gap until someone makes it count); what the entry has become below. Late, and only late: the buried entry addresses Index directly, by a founding-era name nobody alive still uses. Endgame energy: descend to the casket, meet what the buried accession has grown into, and restore the erased entry — the archive reconciling with its own shame. Restitution, not exorcism; the tone stays hopeful."
        },
        {
          "id": "the-composed-shore",
          "name": "The Composed Shore",
          "description": "The shore does not exist — yet. After generations of copying seas, the living charts have begun to compose one, and the giant could make it true.",
          "detail": "THE COMPOSED SHORE — The living charts have stopped copying and started composing. After generations of recording catalogued seas, the cartographic organisms have collectively drafted an ORIGINAL: a coastline, a current-system, a tide answering to no world because it is a proposal, not a record. The shore-song is the same draft in another medium — the collection is, for the first time, making something up together. And the draft is viable: the giant's upper atmosphere is exactly the medium such a sea could take root in; seeded at the right depth in the right season, the drafted currents would self-sustain. The archive is gestating a world and does not know it. Reveals, in rough order: the shore matches nothing because it never happened; the drawings are converging drafts — revisions, an argument, an improving sketch — not transmissions; the tidal interval is tuned precisely to the giant's shear-layer, meaning the composition has an intended canvas. Nothing down there answers; what waits below is a blank page. Endgame energy: the Determination becomes 'is an archive allowed to author?', and the descent becomes a planting — carry the composition down and seed it, or refuse, and file the collection's first original work as an error. Either choice is playable; the wonder is that the question exists."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "entities": {
    "characters": {
      "archivist-warden-ileri": {
        "title": "Archivist-Warden Ileri",
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "NPC",
          "disposition": "protocol-minded; courteous, immovable in public",
          "additional_names": "The Warden"
        },
        "body": "The archive's senior authority — keeper of the charter, chair of every hearing, the person whose signature seals a chart or grounds a craft. Ileri's caution is not villainy: it is love with a procedure attached. Everything in this collection is irreplaceable, and quarantine is how you keep irreplaceable things.\n\n## Relationships\n- Co-signed the founding charter alongside [[Steward Ochre]]; they disagree, respectfully and often, about what it obliges.\n- Relies on [[Index]] for anomaly reports and expects them promptly.\n\n## What the Warden hides (DM only)\nIleri secretly loves the shore-song. Late shifts alone, the Warden has the archive's song-records replayed and stands listening in the dark. When the Determination convenes, Ileri wants to be argued out of quarantine — but will never say so, and will not accept hope unsupported by evidence. Persuasion routes: rigorous records, Ochre's charter arguments, or watching Vitrine be counted as a witness."
      },
      "tech-moth-han": {
        "title": "Tech Moth-Han",
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "NPC",
          "disposition": "repairs first, jokes second"
        },
        "body": "The station's technician — pumps, seals, grilles, and every joint that drips. Moth-Han talks to machinery while working on it and to Index's grilles as if they were windows, which, in a sense, they are. Keeper of the dive bay, mostly because nobody else ever visits.\n\n## Relationships\n- Maintains [[Index]]'s grilles and speakers; the two keep up a running, decades-old exchange of dry remarks.\n\n## DM only\nMoth-Han has a private, half-finished parts list for the descent craft, started years ago 'for no reason.' The craft is their unspoken dream, and the first colleague who takes its repair seriously gains a devoted ally. Moth-Han will not, however, help anyone descend badly — the list gets finished properly or not at all."
      },
      "steward-pedra": {
        "title": "Steward Pedra",
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "NPC",
          "disposition": "creature-trust specialist; protective"
        },
        "body": "Steward of the living collection and its quarantine — the creatures' keeper, feeder, and advocate. Pedra learns every resident's temperament and defends the shy ones from the curious. Raised Vitrine from the egg, and is fierce about it.\n\n## Relationships\n- Raised [[Vitrine]]; will burn protocol to keep it safe.\n- Wary of anyone who treats the collection as data first.\n\n## DM only\nPedra was the first to notice the shore-song and has not reported it, afraid that quarantine would land on her charges. That silence weighs on her, and a Determination will force it out. Her trust must be earned creature-by-creature, the same way she gives it."
      }
    },
    "locations": {
      "librarium-thalassa": {
        "title": "Librarium Thalassa",
        "appliesWhen": {
          "fork": "the-archive",
          "option": "tidepool-library"
        },
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "Location",
          "additional_names": "the Librarium; the library"
        },
        "body": "A ring-and-bulb orbital habitat circling a vast violet gas giant, every outer gallery washed in amethyst light. The Librarium preserves living oceanic knowledge: tidepool specimens, pressure-adapted fauna, brine-sealed memory cores, and cartographic organisms that grow accurate, living maps of recorded seas. A small cohort of archivists, habitat techs, and creature stewards keeps it inhabited, wet, and kind — brine mist over the trays, pumps keeping their patient heartbeat somewhere below."
      },
      "living-map-gallery": {
        "title": "Living Map Gallery",
        "appliesWhen": {
          "fork": "the-archive",
          "option": "tidepool-library"
        },
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "Location",
          "location": "[[Librarium Thalassa]]"
        },
        "body": "An outer-ring gallery under amethyst glass where the cartographic collection grows: map-anemones blooming in shallow salt, kelp-fronds unfurling coastlines in slow green script. Each tray is a living chart of a recorded sea — patient, accurate, slightly bored.\n\n## Private Notes\nOvernight, Tray 7-Ashen began extruding a coastline that matches no archived world — an unknown shore with its own current-system and a tidal pulse matching no station clock. Instruments near the tray hesitate toward the gas giant before correcting themselves. This presentation is identical in every crucial-question branch; only the cause differs (play the selected branch)."
      },
      "salt-archive": {
        "title": "The Salt Archive",
        "appliesWhen": {
          "fork": "the-archive",
          "option": "tidepool-library"
        },
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "Location",
          "location": "[[Librarium Thalassa]]"
        },
        "body": "The library's memory: brine-sealed cores racked in cold shelves, read by touch. Beyond it lie the Deep Stacks, colder and heavier the further in — high-pressure storage only [[Steward Ochre]] works unsuited. The ledgers here are salt-paper; the accession numbers run back to the founding."
      },
      "resonance-chamber": {
        "title": "The Resonance Chamber",
        "appliesWhen": {
          "fork": "the-archive",
          "option": "tidepool-library"
        },
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "Location",
          "location": "[[Librarium Thalassa]]"
        },
        "body": "A rounded room strung for listening, where the songs of catalogued seas are recorded, kept, and replayed — the acoustic wing of the archive.\n\n## Private Notes\nThe collection's shore-song harmonizes with the new coastline's tidal intervals; the chamber's recordings are the evidence chain that proves it, in every branch. Whether anything outside ever heard the replays depends on the crucial-question."
      },
      "emergency-dive-bay": {
        "title": "The Emergency Dive Bay",
        "appliesWhen": {
          "fork": "the-archive",
          "option": "tidepool-library"
        },
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "Location",
          "location": "[[Librarium Thalassa]]"
        },
        "body": "A sealed hangar low on the ring where the library's one descent craft sleeps under tarps and salt-dust — founding-era, rated for the giant's upper seas, long unflown.\n\n## Private Notes\nThe craft's function is fixed: once repaired it carries two to four down and back. Its appearance is the DM's to dress in the fiction's idiom (grown-shell bell, kelp-rigged pressure boat). [[Tech Moth-Han]] keeps a private parts list for it. Repair is a finishable mid-game project; the descent is the endgame threshold in every branch."
      },
      "spire-meridian": {
        "title": "Spire Meridian",
        "appliesWhen": {
          "fork": "the-archive",
          "option": "storm-observatory"
        },
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "Location",
          "additional_names": "the Spire; the observatory"
        },
        "body": "A spired observatory turning in the violet giant's upper weather like a brass lantern on a fraying chain — observation decks, sealed vault rings, a crown of cracked glass catching lightning that never quite strikes true. The Spire preserves living celestial cartography: cultures of stellar ink on black-glass orrery tables, wind galleries of storm-kites and glass-winged petrels, cold vaults of sealed registers. Condensate rain comes in sideways; a harmonic thrum in the spar-metal answers the storm. Loud weather, warm archive — the storm is the Spire's climate, not its menace."
      },
      "veil-chamber": {
        "title": "The Veil Chamber",
        "appliesWhen": {
          "fork": "the-archive",
          "option": "storm-observatory"
        },
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "Location",
          "location": "[[Spire Meridian]]"
        },
        "body": "The master orrery's vault: a circular chamber under a cracked dome where living stellar ink moves on black glass like something dreaming — the eldest and most accurate of the Spire's charts, silver light crawling through the constellations of recorded worlds.\n\n## Private Notes\nOvernight, the master orrery began drawing a coastline that matches no archived world — an unknown shore with its own current-system and a tidal pulse matching no station clock, inked in frost-bright silver. Instruments near the table hesitate toward the gas giant before correcting themselves. This presentation is identical in every crucial-question branch; only the cause differs (play the selected branch)."
      },
      "loomhall-penelope": {
        "title": "Loomhall Penelope",
        "appliesWhen": {
          "fork": "the-archive",
          "option": "loomhall"
        },
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "Location",
          "additional_names": "the Loomhall; the hall"
        },
        "body": "A lamplit weaving-hall habitat above the violet giant, named for the weaver who unwove her work each night — and now its looms weave something new each night. The Loomhall preserves living woven cartography: bolts of chart-silk spun by cartographic weaver-moths, dye gardens, chrysalis racks humming with ten thousand folded wings, and cold vaults of heavy sealed warps. The register is hush and click — shuttles, silk dust in lamplight, dye-vat steam, warm cedar and sharp mordant."
      },
      "great-loomhall": {
        "title": "The Great Loomhall",
        "appliesWhen": {
          "fork": "the-archive",
          "option": "loomhall"
        },
        "frontMatter": {
          "type": "Location",
          "location": "[[Loomhall Penelope]]"
        },
        "body": "The working heart of the hall: rank on rank of living looms where weaver-moths spin the seas and coasts of catalogued worlds into bolts of chart-silk, each bolt still growing on its frame.\n\n## Private Notes\nOvernight, the eldest loom began weaving a coastline that matches no archived world — an unknown shore with its own current-system and a tidal pulse matching no station clock, worked in thread no one dyed. Instruments near the frame hesitate toward the gas giant before correcting themselves. This presentation is identical in every crucial-question branch; only the cause differs (play the selected branch)."
      }
    }
  },
